# Superseded sponsor-normalisation scripts, kept on disk for reference.
# Recoverable from git history if ever needed again.
LEGACY/
rmarkdown/preprocessing.html

# Nightly sponsor resolution runtime state.
#
# config/sponsor_norm_v2/ is otherwise TRACKED on purpose — the registry,
# assignments and mint caches are paid LLM output and the seed state a fresh
# checkout needs. But N_nightly_resolve.R writes its own working files into the
# same directory, and those are per-environment runtime state: a work list, a
# retry ledger, a run history, a crash snapshot. On the server they live in
# $SPONSOR_V2_DIR outside the repo; locally they are test noise, and without
# this a `git add -A` would commit one machine's run history as if it were data.
config/sponsor_norm_v2/N_*.csv
config/sponsor_norm_v2/.snapshot/
data/.sponsor_nightly_failed

# Substance emit by-products, regenerated by E_emit.R on every run.
#
# Neither matches data/*log* or data/*labels*, so without this they sit in
# `git status` as untracked forever and a `git add -A` commits one machine's
# run output to main. They reach Posit the same way the label CSVs do: the
# nightly force-adds substance_rejected.csv onto the deploy branch, which is
# also why it must NOT be tracked on main — two sources for the same file is
# how the deploy branch and main start disagreeing about what the pipeline
# decided. The residue file is diagnostics and has no reader at all.
data/substance_rejected.csv
data/substance_residue.csv

# Per-trial curation overrides, written by curation_app/export.R and force-added
# onto the deploy branch like the label CSVs. Never tracked on main: it is
# reviewer output, not seed state, and config/ would be reverted nightly anyway.
data/trial_overrides.csv

# ── Curation app credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Everything below can carry a credential for the curation
# database or the deploy target, and none of it may ever be committed.
#
# The block above already covers curation_app/{auth,secrets}/, credentials*,
# users.*, sessions and *.sqlite. These are the paths that block missed.

# The FILLED-IN admin seed holds an argon2 password hash. Only the .example
# template, which contains a placeholder, is tracked.
curation_app/sql/seed_admin.sql
curation_app/sql/*_dump.sql
curation_app/sql/reviewers*.sql
!curation_app/sql/*.example

# R session state. .Rhistory captures whatever was typed — and the documented
# way to create an admin hash involves typing a password at the console, so this
# is a realistic leak path, not a theoretical one. .RData serialises live
# objects, which during development includes an open connection string.
.Rhistory
.RData
.Rproj.user/

# rsconnect writes deployment records containing the account token for the
# Posit/shinyapps target. A classic public-repo leak.
rsconnect/
**/rsconnect/

# Catch-alls for anything named after what it holds.
*password*
*passwd*
*_token*
*.token

# Test scripts are never secrets, whatever they are named. tests/no_secrets.sh
# matched the *_secrets* rule above and was silently skipped by `git add` —
# the security test excluded itself from the repo, and only the "paths that
# must stay tracked" check caught it.
!tests/*.sh
!tests/*.R
